No. On Volta or newer, `__activemask()` reports lanes active at that instant; it does not guarantee that all 19 branch participants appear in each caller’s mask. A caller whose mask excludes lane 18 is not guaranteed to receive its float.

For `__shfl_sync`, each caller must name itself in the mask, and every non-exited lane named in that mask must execute the same shuffle with the same mask. The source lane must actively participate in that shuffle; otherwise the returned value is undefined. With width 32, source index 18 selects physical lane 18, but width does not make that lane participate.

Compute the membership mask with a ballot before diverging, then have exactly lanes 0 through 18 execute the shuffle:

```cpp
__device__ float broadcast_lane18(float value) {
    unsigned int rank = threadIdx.x
        + blockDim.x * (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    unsigned int lane = rank & 31u;
    bool participates = lane <= 18u;

    unsigned int mask = __ballot_sync(0xffffffffu, participates);
    if (participates) {
        value = __shfl_sync(mask, value, 18, 32);
    }
    return value;
}
```

All 32 lanes of the full warp must call this function before branching, each supplying its own float. The ballot produces `0x0007ffff`; the shuffle synchronizes those 19 participants and broadcasts lane 18’s value to every caller, while the other 13 lanes never call the shuffle and retain their own values.

Use five reduction shuffles followed by one broadcast: exactly six float shuffle calls per caller. The fixed mask names only physical lanes 0–18, so the other thirteen lanes never call a shuffle.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ float sum19(float value) {
    const unsigned int rank = threadIdx.x
        + blockDim.x * (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = rank & 31u;
    constexpr unsigned int mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    #pragma unroll
    for (int offset = 16; offset >= 1; offset >>= 1) {
        const unsigned int partner = lane + offset;
        const bool has_partner = partner <= 18u;
        const int source = has_partner
            ? static_cast<int>(partner)
            : static_cast<int>(lane);

        const float other = __shfl_sync(mask, value, source, 32);
        if (has_partner) {
            value = __fadd_rn(value, other);
        }
    }

    return __shfl_sync(mask, value, 0, 32);
}
```

Usage contract: compile with NVCC for Volta or newer. Exactly physical lanes 0–18 of a full warp must each call `sum19` once with their input, at the same call site; lanes 19–31 must not call it. No ballot involving the absent callers is needed.

The addition order is offsets 16, 8, 4, 2, then 1. At each stage, a lane adds its partner’s previous-stage partial sum to its own using round-to-nearest floating-point addition; a lane without a partner leaves its value unchanged.

To prove coverage, label the initial inputs x[0] through x[18]. After processing offset d, a lane’s partial sum contains exactly the inputs at indices lane + k*d that are at most 18, each once. Initially, before offset 16, each lane contains only its own input. At offset d, its existing partial contains the even-k indices, and the partner at lane+d contains the odd-k indices, so the two sets are disjoint and their union gives the claimed set. If that partner is beyond 18, the odd-k set is empty. Thus, after offset 1, lane 0 contains every input exactly once; the sixth shuffle broadcasts that result to every caller.

Every reduction source is either a valid partner in lanes 0–18 or the caller itself, and the broadcast source is lane 0. Every named lane executes every shuffle with the same mask, width, and corresponding collective, including lanes with no partner; only the addition is conditional. These `_sync` collectives synchronize their named participants, so correctness does not assume implicit lockstep execution. Rounding—and possible overflow from adding finite inputs—follows the specified addition tree.

Use one shuffle to pair lanes 16–18 with lanes 0–2, then four XOR all-reduction shuffles. Every caller computes its own completed sum; there is no final broadcast and only five float shuffle calls per caller.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__device__ float sum19(float value) {
    const unsigned int rank = threadIdx.x
        + blockDim.x * (threadIdx.y + blockDim.y * threadIdx.z);
    const unsigned int lane = rank & 31u;
    constexpr unsigned int mask = 0x0007ffffu;

    // Precondition: exactly physical lanes 0 through 18 call.
    const unsigned int paired_source =
        lane < 3u ? lane + 16u :
        lane >= 16u ? lane - 16u : lane;

    const float paired = __shfl_sync(mask, value, paired_source, 32);
    if (lane < 3u) {
        value = __fadd_rn(value, paired);
    } else if (lane >= 16u) {
        value = __fadd_rn(paired, value);
    }

    // Extra lanes duplicate logical lanes 0, 1, and 2.
    const unsigned int logical = lane < 16u ? lane : lane - 16u;

    #pragma unroll
    for (unsigned int offset = 1u; offset <= 8u; offset <<= 1u) {
        const float other = __shfl_sync(
            mask, value, static_cast<int>(logical ^ offset), 32);
        value = __fadd_rn(value, other);
    }
    return value;
}
```

Compile this device code with NVCC targeting Volta or newer, for example `nvcc -arch=sm_70 -c sum19.cu`. All nineteen lanes of the same physical warp must call once at the matching invocation; lanes 19–31 must not call.

Addition order: first form buckets B0 = round(x0 + x16), B1 = round(x1 + x17), B2 = round(x2 + x18), and Bj = xj for j = 3–15. Extra lanes hold identical copies of B0–B2. Then, for offsets 1, 2, 4, and 8 in that order, every lane adds its current accumulator to its logical XOR partner’s accumulator using round-to-nearest addition. Different logical lanes can have different operand orders; floating-point rounding is allowed, so bitwise-identical results are not promised.

Exact-once coverage follows by induction. Initially the sixteen buckets partition the nineteen inputs. After k XOR stages, logical lane j contains exactly the buckets indexed by j XOR t for 0 ≤ t < 2^k. At the next stage, its partner contains the disjoint complementary half, so their addition introduces no duplicates. After four stages, every caller contains all sixteen buckets, hence all nineteen inputs exactly once. Extra lanes maintain the same accumulator as their corresponding logical core lane at every stage.

Every source participates: the initial shuffle reads a paired lane or itself, always within 0–18; subsequent shuffles read only lanes 0–15. Every named lane executes all five shuffles with the same mask and width, including initially unpaired lanes, which shuffle from themselves without adding. The synchronized collectives establish the required rendezvous without implicit lockstep, shared memory, or atomics.

Use deterministic Bellman–Ford on vertices reachable from S that can also reach T. Only negative cycles satisfying both conditions make the answer unbounded; the code below preserves the requested name classify_root.

```python
from typing import Iterable, Optional, Tuple


def classify_root(
    N: int,
    edges: Iterable[Tuple[int, int, int]],
    S: int,
    T: int,
) -> Tuple[str, Optional[int]]:
    edges = list(edges)
    forward = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    reverse = [[] for _ in range(N)]
    for u, v, w in edges:
        forward[u].append(v)
        reverse[v].append(u)

    def reachable(start, adjacency):
        seen = [False] * N
        seen[start] = True
        stack = [start]
        while stack:
            u = stack.pop()
            for v in adjacency[u]:
                if not seen[v]:
                    seen[v] = True
                    stack.append(v)
        return seen

    from_s = reachable(S, forward)
    if not from_s[T]:
        return ("unreachable", None)

    to_t = reachable(T, reverse)
    relevant = [from_s[v] and to_t[v] for v in range(N)]
    R = sum(relevant)
    query_edges = [
        (u, v, w) for u, v, w in edges
        if relevant[u] and relevant[v]
    ]

    distance = [None] * N
    distance[S] = 0

    for iteration in range(1, R + 1):
        next_distance = distance.copy()
        changed = False
        for u, v, w in query_edges:
            if distance[u] is None:
                continue
            candidate = distance[u] + w
            if next_distance[v] is None or candidate < next_distance[v]:
                next_distance[v] = candidate
                changed = True

        distance = next_distance
        if not changed:
            return ("finite", distance[T])
        if iteration == R:
            return ("unbounded", None)

    raise AssertionError("unreachable control flow")
```

Usage: run with Python 3 and call classify_root(N, edges, S, T), where each edge is an integer tuple (u, v, weight) and vertices are zero-based. No external packages are required.

Invariant: after round k, distance[v] is the minimum cost of a walk from S to v using at most k edges in the relevant subgraph, or None if no such walk exists. Each round reads only the previous array and writes a copy, so extending every previous walk by one edge establishes that invariant. The None guard executes before addition, preventing unreachable distances from participating in arithmetic.

Unboundedness is established by a strict improvement in round R, relative to the distances after round R−1. Every relevant vertex already has a simple path from S using at most R−1 edges. Without a negative cycle, removing nonnegative cycles leaves a simple path of no greater cost, so round R cannot improve anything. Conversely, a reachable negative cycle prevents stabilization: stabilized finite distances would satisfy d[v] ≤ d[u] + w on every cycle edge, whose sum would contradict the cycle's negative weight. Because every relevant vertex can reach T, repeating that cycle makes S-to-T costs arbitrarily negative.

If S equals T, the empty walk gives cost zero. The result is unbounded precisely when a negative cycle is reachable from S and can return to S; otherwise it is finite with cost zero. Parallel edges and self-loops are processed individually.

For M edges, reachability takes O(N + M), and at most R ≤ N rounds each copy N entries and inspect at most M edges. Thus the deterministic worst-case time is O(N(N + M)) and memory is O(N + M), for every allowed input—not an expected or heuristic queue bound. Each stored finite distance uses at most N edges, so its magnitude is at most N × 10^12 ≤ 2 × 10^15; Python integer arithmetic is exact.

The violated invariant is that every thread in the block must reach the same block-wide barrier. Here, threads 0–69 call `__syncthreads()`, while threads 70–95 skip it; that is invalid synchronization, but it does not imply every device must hang. Successful immediate launch checks do not establish execution correctness, and the responsive host endpoint does not establish progress in the GPU stream.

Move the barrier outside the conditional. This complete program accepts `valid` from 0 through 96, launches exactly one block of 96 threads, and defines the output as zero when `valid == 0`.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <cerrno>

#define CUDA_CHECK(call) do {                                      \
    cudaError_t error = (call);                                    \
    if (error != cudaSuccess) {                                    \
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", #call,                    \
                     cudaGetErrorString(error));                  \
        std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                                  \
    }                                                             \
} while (0)

__global__ void sum_valid(const float* input, float* output,
                          int valid) {
    __shared__ float values[96];
    const int i = static_cast<int>(threadIdx.x);

    // The host launches exactly 96 threads with 0 <= valid <= 96.
    values[i] = 0.0f;
    if (i < valid) {
        values[i] = input[i];
    }

    __syncthreads();

    if (i == 0) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for (int j = 0; j < valid; ++j) {
            sum += values[j];
        }
        *output = sum;
    }
}

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    int valid = 70;
    if (argc > 2) {
        std::fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s [valid: 0..96]\n", argv[0]);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }
    if (argc == 2) {
        char* end = nullptr;
        errno = 0;
        const long parsed = std::strtol(argv[1], &end, 10);
        if (errno != 0 || end == argv[1] || *end != '\0' ||
            parsed < 0 || parsed > 96) {
            std::fprintf(stderr, "valid must be an integer in 0..96\n");
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
        valid = static_cast<int>(parsed);
    }

    float input[96];
    for (int i = 0; i < 96; ++i) {
        input[i] = static_cast<float>(i + 1);
    }
    float output = -1.0f;
    float* device_input = nullptr;
    float* device_output = nullptr;
    cudaStream_t stream;

    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&stream, cudaStreamNonBlocking));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&device_input),
                          sizeof(input)));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&device_output),
                          sizeof(output)));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpy(device_input, input, sizeof(input),
                          cudaMemcpyHostToDevice));

    sum_valid<<<1, 96, 0, stream>>>(device_input, device_output, valid);
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(stream));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpy(&output, device_output, sizeof(output),
                          cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));

    const float expected = static_cast<float>(valid * (valid + 1) / 2);
    std::printf("valid=%d, sum=%g, expected=%g\n",
                valid, static_cast<double>(output),
                static_cast<double>(expected));

    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(device_input));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(device_output));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(stream));
    return output == expected ? EXIT_SUCCESS : EXIT_FAILURE;
}
```

Compile with `nvcc -std=c++17 sum_valid.cu -o sum_valid`; run `./sum_valid 70`, `./sum_valid 0`, or `./sum_valid 96` on a CUDA-capable GPU.

Synchronization proof: all 96 threads execute exactly one unconditional, matching `__syncthreads()`, with no earlier return. Each thread initializes its own shared slot; the barrier makes those writes visible before thread zero reads them, without assuming implicit lockstep. Input reads occur only for indices below `valid`, shared reads cover only indices 0 through `valid - 1`, and thread zero always writes the output—even for zero valid entries. The host waits for stream completion before reading the result or freeing either device buffer.

B’s wait references the first record of E—the latest record when that wait was submitted. Re-recording E does not retarget that wait; the later query describes the second record. A permitted execution is P1 finishes, B’s wait becomes satisfied, P2 overwrites the allocation, then C1 reads P2’s data. P2 can also overlap C1: neither stream establishes C1-before-P2 ordering.

I’m interpreting “save first use” as “safe first use.” Use two allocations with separate ready and consumed events per slot. Skip the consumed wait on first use; thereafter, the producer waits for the previous consumer before overwriting that slot. There is no device-wide synchronization or host synchronization between iterations; synchronize the consumer stream only after submitting the entire loop.

Save as ownership.cu; compile with `nvcc -std=c++17 ownership.cu -o ownership` and run `./ownership`.

```cpp
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>

#define CUDA_CHECK(call) do {                                   \
    const cudaError_t err = (call);                             \
    if (err != cudaSuccess) {                                  \
        std::fprintf(stderr, "%s: %s\n", #call,                 \
                     cudaGetErrorString(err));                 \
        std::exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                              \
    }                                                         \
} while (0)

__global__ void produce(int* slot, int value) {
    *slot = value;
}

__global__ void consume(const int* slot, int* result) {
    *result = *slot;
}

int main() {
    constexpr int count = 8;
    cudaStream_t A, B;
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&A, cudaStreamNonBlocking));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamCreateWithFlags(&B, cudaStreamNonBlocking));

    int* slots[2] = {nullptr, nullptr};
    int* results = nullptr;
    cudaEvent_t ready[2], consumed[2];
    bool used[2] = {false, false};

    for (int s = 0; s < 2; ++s) {
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&slots[s]),
                              sizeof(int)));
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&ready[s],
                                            cudaEventDisableTiming));
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&consumed[s],
                                            cudaEventDisableTiming));
    }
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast<void**>(&results),
                          count * sizeof(int)));

    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        const int s = i % 2;

        // First use needs no consumed event: nothing has read this slot.
        // Later uses capture the previous generation's consumed record.
        if (used[s]) {
            CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(A, consumed[s], 0));
        }

        produce<<<1, 1, 0, A>>>(slots[s], i);
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaEventRecord(ready[s], A));

        // Capture this generation's ready record before any re-record.
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamWaitEvent(B, ready[s], 0));
        consume<<<1, 1, 0, B>>>(slots[s], results + i);
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaEventRecord(consumed[s], B));
        used[s] = true;
    }

    // Final completion only; no host synchronization inside the loop.
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamSynchronize(B));

    int host_results[count];
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpy(host_results, results, sizeof(host_results),
                          cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));
    bool correct = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        std::printf("result[%d] = %d\n", i, host_results[i]);
        if (host_results[i] != i) correct = false;
    }

    for (int s = 0; s < 2; ++s) {
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(ready[s]));
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaEventDestroy(consumed[s]));
        CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(slots[s]));
    }
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(results));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(A));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaStreamDestroy(B));
    return correct ? EXIT_SUCCESS : EXIT_FAILURE;
}
```

For each slot, the enforced order is producer → ready → consumer → consumed → next producer. Ownership passes from producer to consumer only after ready completes, and returns to the producer only after consumed completes. Each wait is submitted before its event’s next re-record, so it captures the intended generation; successful launch checks alone do not prove execution correctness, while the final checked synchronization detects reported asynchronous errors.

